      <h2>Austin Tiny Home Builders for Small Lots and Big Plans</h2>
      <p>Few Texas cities have changed their housing rules as much as Austin has in recent years, and that has made the city one of the most interesting places to build small. Homeowners who once assumed a second home on their lot was off the table are now sketching backyard cottages, and buyers priced out of a full-size house are looking at tiny homes as a real path to ownership. As tiny home builders in Austin, Cowboy Tiny Homes helps you turn that new flexibility into a home that is well designed, permit-ready and worth what you spend.</p>
      <p>Austin projects reward careful planning. Lots are expensive, trees are protected, impervious cover is limited and neighbors notice what goes up next door. A small home that looks good, respects the street and fits the rules gets approved faster and adds more value.</p>

      <h2>What Austin's HOME Changes Mean for You</h2>
      <p>Through the HOME (Home Options for Families and Empowerment) initiative, Austin updated its land development code so that most lots zoned for single-family homes can now have up to three housing units, and a second phase reduced the minimum lot size for a single home. In practice, that opens the door to a backyard ADU, a second detached home or a small home on a newly split lot in places where it was not possible before.</p>
      <p>Other rules still shape the design: impervious cover limits, setbacks, height, protected and heritage tree ordinances, compatibility standards near certain properties, drainage, and any deed restrictions or HOA covenants on your lot. Some details of the code continue to be refined, so confirm the current requirements with the City of Austin Development Services Department for your address.</p>
      <div class="callout"><p><strong>We check before we draw.</strong> The feasibility study in every Cowboy Tiny Homes design package looks at your zoning, impervious cover, trees, setbacks and utilities so the design starts from what is buildable. For background, see our <a href="/guides/tiny-house-laws-texas/">Texas tiny house laws guide</a>.</p></div>

