      <h2>Designing Small for Central Texas</h2>
      <p>Austin's long, hot summers and sudden storms shape every Cowboy Tiny Homes design here. We favor generous roof overhangs and covered porches for shade, high-performance windows placed away from harsh west light, spray-foam or continuous exterior insulation, and right-sized mini-split heat pumps. Standing-seam metal roofs handle hail and reflect heat well.</p>
      <p>West of MoPac and into the Hill Country, shallow limestone and steep lots affect foundations and utility runs. East of I-35, expansive clay soils call for engineered slabs or pier-and-beam construction. On tree-heavy lots, we can site the home and choose foundation types that work around critical root zones — often the difference between a quick approval and a redesign.</p>

      <h2>Making an Austin ADU Pay for Itself</h2>
      <p>Strong rental demand is one of the biggest reasons Austin homeowners call us. A well-planned one-bedroom ADU can help cover a mortgage or property taxes, and a flexible layout lets it serve family later. Keep in mind that short-term rentals are regulated separately by the city and require their own licensing, so model long-term rent as your baseline. Our <a href="/tools/adu-rental-income-calculator/">ADU rental income calculator</a> shows cash flow, ROI and payback, and the <a href="/tools/tiny-home-cost-calculator/">tiny home cost calculator</a> gives you a build budget to plug in.</p>

      <h2>Austin-Area Communities We Serve</h2>
      <p>Cowboy Tiny Homes builds across <strong>Travis County</strong> — including East Austin, South Austin, Pflugerville, Manor and Lakeway — as well as Round Rock, Georgetown, Cedar Park, Leander and Hutto in <strong>Williamson County</strong>, and San Marcos, Kyle, Buda, Dripping Springs and Wimberley in <strong>Hays County</strong>. Bastrop, Lockhart and Burnet County land is on the list too.</p>

      <h2>Your Austin Project Checklist</h2>
      <ul class="checklist">
        <li>Confirm your zoning and how many units your lot allows</li>
        <li>Check impervious cover, setbacks and protected trees</li>
        <li>Review deed restrictions and HOA rules</li>
        <li>Locate water, wastewater and electric connections</li>
        <li>Set a budget that includes utilities and site work</li>
        <li>Get permit-ready drawings from an experienced small-home designer</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Want help with any of it? Start with a free consult, or see <a href="/tiny-house-design-services/">tiny house design services</a> if you already have a builder.</p>
